1. Setup: Load an image and add Gaussian noise
The code loads the original image and adds Gaussian noise to simulate a noisy image. 
This noisy image is used to test the denoising methods.

2. Traditional Filter (Adaptive Wiener Filter)
The code applies an adaptive Wiener filter to reduce noise.
It estimates local image statistics and adjusts the filtering based on the surrounding pixels.

3. AI Filter (Pre-trained DnCNN)
The code uses a pretrained DnCNN deep-learning network to remove noise from the image. 
The network has learned to recognise and remove noise while preserving image details.

4. Quantitative Evaluation (PSNR and SSIM)
The code calculates PSNR and SSIM to compare the denoised images with the original image. 
Higher PSNR and SSIM values generally indicate better image quality and similarity.

5. Visual Observation
The code displays the original, noisy and denoised images side by side. 
This makes it possible to visually compare how well the Wiener filter and DnCNN remove noise and preserve image details.
